<h2>The petition and the clause</h2>
<p>The petitioner invoked arbitration under Clause 8 of an Indirect Vendor Agreement dated 9 January 2023, claiming approximately Rs. 2.72 crore. The clause is a textbook tiered-dispute provision: an attempt at amicable settlement within thirty days of notice; failing that, arbitration before a sole arbitrator appointed mutually, and if the parties cannot agree within fifteen days, appointment in accordance with the Act; the seat at Delhi, proceedings in English, each party initially bearing its own costs subject to the award, interim injunctive relief preserved, and exclusive jurisdiction of New Delhi courts for legal proceedings. Mutual appointment having failed, the Section 11(6) petition followed. Goodyear&rsquo;s counsel appeared and recorded no objection to the Court appointing an independent arbitrator.</p>

<h2>The order in four steps</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Scope confined.</strong> Citing Krish Spinning, the Court restated that <em>&ldquo;the scope of enquiry is confined to examining the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Existence undisputed.</strong> <em>&ldquo;There is no dispute with regard to the existence of a valid arbitration clause between the parties&rdquo;</em> — with both sides agreeing to a reference, no impediment remained.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appointment with timelines.</strong> A sole arbitrator was named, directed to enter upon the reference within two weeks and to make the disclosures required by Section 12(2) within two weeks thereafter.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Institutional frame.</strong> The arbitration will run under the aegis and rules of the DIAC, with fees under its schedule; all pleas, including arbitrability and jurisdiction, remain open before the tribunal.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why routine orders like this still repay reading</h2>
<p>Three features are worth noting for commercial parties. First, the tiered clause worked exactly as designed: the pre-arbitration negotiation window and the fifteen-day mutual-appointment window each expired, and the court stepped in only at the point the clause itself appointed it to. Vendors and principals drafting supply or distribution agreements should fix these windows in days, as here, because open-ended &ldquo;amicable settlement&rdquo; language breeds maintainability objections. Second, the DIAC reference reflects the Delhi High Court&rsquo;s settled preference for institutional administration of court-appointed arbitrations — parties get a fee schedule, case management and disclosure enforcement without further court visits. Third, the no-objection strategy deserves thought on the respondent side: conceding the appointment costs nothing where the clause is undeniable, preserves every substantive defence under Section 16, and avoids an adverse costs narrative at the first hearing.</p>

<h2>The Section 11 landscape in brief</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Question raised at referral</th><th>Who decides it now</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Does an arbitration agreement exist, prima facie?</td><td>The referral court — the only question it decides</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the claim time-barred or deadwood?</td><td>The arbitral tribunal (Krish Spinning)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the dispute arbitrable at all?</td><td>The tribunal under Section 16, kept open here in terms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Are the claims good on merits?</td><td>The tribunal, uninfluenced by any referral observation</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">Appointment orders under Section 11(6) decide nothing on the merits; every substantive plea remains open before the arbitral tribunal. Case details are as recorded in the order dated 18 November 2026.</div>
